.SH NAME
ktm-guide-defer-and-scoped \- defer and scoped (chapter 16 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBdefer\fR runs a statement when its block is left, however it is left: the end of the block, a \fBreturn\fR, a \fBbreak\fR, or an error passing through. \fBscoped\fR in front of a declaration frees the variable the same way. Both keep the clean-up next to the code that needs it, so it can't be forgotten on one of the ways out. This chapter covers \fBdefer\fR, the order defers run in, what they see, and \fBscoped\fR\&.
.SH defer
.PP
\fBdefer statement;\fR registers the statement to run when the enclosing \fB{ }\fR block is left:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    defer printf("last\en");
    printf("first\en");
    printf("second\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
first
second
last
.EE
.RE
.PP
A block after \fBdefer\fR defers all of it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

void work() {
    u8 *buf = mem.alloc(64);
    defer {
        mem.free(buf);
        printf("freed\en");
    }
    printf("working\en");
}

i32 main() {
    work();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
working
freed
.EE
.RE
.PP
The usual pattern is to defer the release right after acquiring, so the two sit together:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

i64 sumSquares(i64 n) {
    i64 *squares = mem.alloc(n * sizeof(i64));
    defer mem.free(squares);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        squares[i] = i * i;
    }
    i64 total = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        total += squares[i];
    }
    return total;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", sumSquares(5));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
30
.EE
.RE
.SH Every way out
.PP
A deferred statement runs at the end of its block, on a \fBreturn\fR, on \fBbreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR, on a \fBgoto\fR out of the block, and when a thrown error passes through:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { negative };

i64 check(i64 n) {
    defer printf("  check(%d) done\en", n);
    if (n < 0) {
        throw(Err.negative);
    }
    if (n == 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    return n * 2;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", check(5));
    printf("%d\en", check(0));
    try {
        check(\-1);
    } catch {
        printf("caught\en");
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
  check(5) done
10
  check(0) done
0
  check(\-1) done
caught
.EE
.RE
.PP
With several early returns, one \fBdefer\fR replaces a clean-up before each of them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

i64 firstEven(i64[] input) {
    i64 *copy = mem.alloc(input.len * sizeof(i64));
    defer {
        mem.free(copy);
        printf("(copy freed)\en");
    }
    for (i64 i = 0; i < input.len; i++) {
        copy[i] = input[i];
        if (copy[i] % 2 == 0) {
            return copy[i];
        }
    }
    return \-1;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 a[4] = {3, 5, 8, 9};
    i64 b[2] = {1, 3};
    printf("%d\en", firstEven(a));
    printf("%d\en", firstEven(b));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
(copy freed)
8
(copy freed)
\-1
.EE
.RE
.SH The block is the scope
.PP
A defer belongs to the \fB{ }\fR block it is written in, not to the function. In a loop body it runs at the end of every iteration, \fBcontinue\fR included:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        defer printf("end of %d\en", i);
        if (i == 1) {
            continue;
        }
        printf("body %d\en", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
body 0
end of 0
end of 1
body 2
end of 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
A plain block limits it too:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    printf("before\en");
    {
        defer printf("inner block left\en");
        printf("inside\en");
    }
    printf("after\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
before
inside
inner block left
after
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBbreak\fR leaves the loop's body, so its defers run first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        defer printf("done with %d\en", i);
        if (i == 2) {
            break;
        }
    }
    printf("loop over\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
done with 0
done with 1
done with 2
loop over
.EE
.RE
.SH Order
.PP
Several defers in a block run in reverse: the last one registered runs first. Inner blocks finish before outer ones.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    defer printf("outer\en");
    {
        defer printf("1st registered, runs last\en");
        defer printf("2nd registered, runs first\en");
    }
    printf("between\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2nd registered, runs first
1st registered, runs last
between
outer
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reverse order is what nested resources need: what was acquired last is released first.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void open(u8 *what) {
    printf("open %s\en", what);
}

void close(u8 *what) {
    printf("close %s\en", what);
}

i32 main() {
    open("database");
    defer close("database");
    open("transaction");
    defer close("transaction");
    printf("work\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
open database
open transaction
work
close transaction
close database
.EE
.RE
.SH What a defer sees
.PP
The deferred statement runs later, as written: it sees the values variables have when the block ends, not when the \fBdefer\fR was written.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    i64 x = 1;
    {
        defer printf("x is %d\en", x);
        x = 2;
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
x is 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBreturn\fR evaluates its value before the defers run, so a function can return something its defer then frees, as long as it isn't a pointer into what is freed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

i64 first(i64 n) {
    i64 *buf = mem.alloc(n * sizeof(i64));
    defer mem.free(buf);
    buf[0] = 42;
    return buf[0];
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", first(4));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42
.EE
.RE
.SH Only if reached
.PP
A \fBdefer\fR is registered when execution reaches it. One written after an early return doesn't run on that return:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void f(bool early) {
    if (early) {
        return;
    }
    defer printf("deferred\en");
    printf("late part\en");
}

i32 main() {
    f(true);
    f(false);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
late part
deferred
.EE
.RE
.PP
So a defer that frees something goes after the check that it was acquired:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

bool fill(i64 n) {
    if (n <= 0) {
        return false;
    }
    u8 *buf = mem.alloc(n);
    defer {
        mem.free(buf);
        printf("freed %d\en", n);
    }
    printf("filled %d\en", n);
    return true;
}

i32 main() {
    fill(0);
    fill(16);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
filled 16
freed 16
.EE
.RE
.SH What a deferred statement can't do
.PP
A deferred statement can't leave itself with \fBreturn\fR, \fBbreak\fR, \fBcontinue\fR or \fBgoto\fR (a loop inside it keeps its own \fBbreak\fR):
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        defer {
            break;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
`break` can't leave a deferred statement
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A \fBgoto\fR can't jump past a \fBdefer\fR into the rest of its block.
.IP \(bu 2
A throw from a deferred statement goes on like any other throw.
.IP \(bu 2
Returning from \fBmain\fR runs its defers; \fBos.exit()\fR and a crash don't.
.IP \(bu 2
Switching between coroutines doesn't leave a block, so it runs no defers.
.IP \(bu 2
Only functions that use \fBdefer\fR pay for it.
.SH scoped
.PP
\fBscoped\fR in front of a declaration means \fBdefer name.free();\fR right after it: the variable is freed when its block ends.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

i32 main() {
    scoped String name = str.from("Ada");
    name.append(" Lovelace");
    printf("%.*s\en", name.len, name.ptr);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Ada Lovelace
.EE
.RE
.PP
It works for a \fBString\fR, any slice the library allocated, and any struct with a \fBfree()\fR method that takes no arguments, or a pointer to such a struct:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

struct Conn { i64 fd; };

void Conn.free() {
    printf("closing %d\en", self.fd);
}

i32 main() {
    scoped String name = str.from("server");
    scoped u8[][] words = str.split("a b c", " ");
    scoped Conn c = {7};
    printf("%.*s has %d words\en", name.len, name.ptr, words.len);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
server has 3 words
closing 7
.EE
.RE
.PP
The library's containers all have \fBfree()\fR, so \fBscoped\fR is the usual way to hold them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import map, set, vec;

i32 main() {
    scoped vec.Vec<i64> v = vec.new<i64>();
    scoped map.Map<u8 *, i64> m = map.new<u8 *, i64>();
    scoped set.Set<i64> s = set.new<i64>();
    v.push(1);
    v.push(2);
    m.set("a", 1);
    s.add(1);
    printf("%d %d %d\en", v.len, m.len, s.len);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 1 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Like a \fBdefer\fR, a \fBscoped\fR variable is freed on every way out of its block, a thrown error included:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Res { u8 *name; };

void Res.free() {
    printf("released %s\en", self.name);
}

enum Err { bad };

void use() {
    scoped Res r = {"lock"};
    throw(Err.bad, "failed while holding it");
}

i32 main() {
    try {
        use();
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("caught: %s\en", e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
released lock
caught: failed while holding it
.EE
.RE
.SS Without a value
.PP
A \fBscoped\fR variable can be declared without a value and filled in later. Until then it is empty (every local starts zeroed), and freeing an empty \fBString\fR, slice or container does nothing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

i32 main() {
    scoped String s;
    if (s.len == 0) {
        s = str.from("filled later");
    }
    printf("%.*s\en", s.len, s.ptr);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
filled later
.EE
.RE
.PP
A struct marked \fB@json\fR or \fB@yaml\fR that has no \fBfree()\fR of its own is freed with the \fBfreeDecoded()\fR those attributes generate (see Attributes (see ktm-guide-attributes(7))), so a value decoded later is freed too:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json;

@json struct User {
    String name;
    i64 age;
};

i32 main() {
    scoped User u;
    u = json.decode<User>("{\e"name\e": \e"ada\e", \e"age\e": 36}");
    printf("%.*s is %d\en", u.name.len, u.name.ptr, u.age);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
ada is 36
.EE
.RE
.PP
A \fBscoped\fR pointer with no value is \fBnull\fR, and is freed only if it holds something by the end of its block.
.SS scoped and returning
.PP
Returning a \fBscoped\fR variable is an error: it would be freed as the function returns. A function that hands a value to its caller declares it without \fBscoped\fR, and the caller decides:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

String make() {
    scoped String s = str.from("x");
    return s;
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
is declared `scoped`, so it is freed when this function returns
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

String greeting(u8[] who) {
    String s = str.format("hello, %.*s", who.len, who.ptr);
    return s;
}

i32 main() {
    scoped String g = greeting("ada");
    printf("%.*s\en", g.len, g.ptr);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
hello, ada
.EE
.RE
.PP
A type with no \fBfree()\fR can't be declared \fBscoped\fR:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x, y; };

i32 main() {
    scoped Point p = {1, 2};
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
has no `free()` that takes no arguments
.EE
.RE
.SS scoped rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBscoped T x = value;\fR is \fBT x = value; defer x.free();\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
It takes a \fBString\fR, a slice, or a struct (or pointer to one) with a \fBfree()\fR that takes no arguments; an \fB@json\fR/\fB@yaml\fR struct without one uses \fBfreeDecoded()\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Without a value the variable starts empty and is still freed at the end.
.IP \(bu 2
Returning a \fBscoped\fR variable is an error; a copy of it shares what it points to and is freed with it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBscoped\fR goes on a local variable, not a field. It isn't a reserved word: a variable can be called \fBscoped\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Until v0.24.0 it was spelled \fBad\fR\&. Since v0.25.0 \fBad\fR is an ordinary name, and in front of a declaration an error that says to write \fBscoped\fR\&.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdefer statement;\fR or \fBdefer { ... }\fR runs when its enclosing block is left, by any way: the end, \fBreturn\fR, \fBbreak\fR, \fBcontinue\fR, \fBgoto\fR, or a throw passing through.
.IP \(bu 2
Defers of a block run last first; inner blocks before outer ones.
.IP \(bu 2
A defer is registered when reached, and runs with the values of the moment it runs.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreturn\fR evaluates its value before the defers run.
.IP \(bu 2
A deferred statement can't \fBreturn\fR, \fBbreak\fR, \fBcontinue\fR or \fBgoto\fR out of itself.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBos.exit()\fR and crashes don't run defers; returning from \fBmain\fR does.
